# Git workflow

Prefer **one branch per feature** so each change can get its own **Vercel Preview URL**. Keep **`main` as production**. Do **not** use a shared staging branch as the default review env.

**Do not commit or push unless the user explicitly asks in this conversation.** See `.cursor/rules/no-unsolicited-ship.mdc`. The rest of this file is how to work when they do ask.

**Before any merge / push to `main`:** run `npm run check`, `npm run test`, and `npm run build:check`. Only ship when all three pass — do not push hoping CI will catch failures.

## Ship path vs review path

| Path | What | Deploys |
| ---- | ---- | ------- |
| **Review** | Feature branch (`cursor/…` or similar), optionally a PR | Vercel **Preview** |
| **Ship** | Merge into `main` (only when John asks) | Vercel **Production** → gridsolutions.app |

Flow:

```
Issue → feature branch → work → (when asked) push branch / open PR
  → Vercel Preview URL for review
  → (when John asks to ship) quality gates → merge to main → Production
```

- Open a PR when it helps review or surfaces the preview link. PRs are a review/preview tool, not a long-lived merge bureaucracy.
- Cloud agents must **not** push or merge to `main` unless John explicitly asks to ship/merge.
- Never create a long-lived shared staging branch or Supabase Branching preview as the default review env.
- Agent desktop / Live and `npm run dev` on hosted Dev remain valid review paths alongside Preview.

## Issues first

Track work with GitHub issues (`gh`). Before committing (only after the user asked to commit):

1. If the user named an issue (`#123`, a URL, or a title), use that.
2. Otherwise search: `gh issue list --state open --limit 30`
3. If none fits, create one: `gh issue create --title "..." --body "..."`
4. Put that issue number in the commit.

Do not open a second issue for work already covered by an open one.

## Branching

1. `git fetch origin main` / `git pull origin main` (or rebase onto latest `main`) before starting.
2. Create a **feature branch** from `main`, e.g. `cursor/<short-description>` (cloud agents often use a runtime suffix — that is fine).
3. Do all work on that branch. Do not commit directly on `main` unless John explicitly asks for a main-only hotfix.
4. When asked to push for preview/review: `git push -u origin HEAD` (feature branch only). Share the Vercel Preview URL / PR link.
5. When asked to **ship**: merge the feature branch into `main` (via PR merge or local merge), run quality gates, then push `main`. Do **not** push a standalone patch `APP_VERSION` bump — the main bump Action handles that. Only bump major/minor in the feature PR when updating What's New. Never force-push `main` unless explicitly asked.

## Commits

Large commits are fine. Prefer one commit per issue (or one commit that closes the issues this change finishes).

Every commit must reference the issue(s):

```
Add customer CSV export

Closes #142
```

- Completes the issue → `Closes #N` or `Fixes #N` (auto-closes when the commit lands on `main`)
- Partial work → `Refs #N` (leave the issue open)
- Multiple issues → `Closes #12, #34`

Subject line: what changed and why. Not a file list.

## Push (only when the user asks)

| Ask | Action |
| --- | ------ |
| Push / commit for preview or review | Push the **feature branch** only (`git push -u origin HEAD`). Optionally open/update a PR. Does **not** deploy production. |
| Ship / merge / push to production | Quality gates → merge to **`main`** → `git push origin main` → Production. Bump app version in the same ship when the repo’s version pattern applies. |

Never force-push `main` unless the user explicitly asks.
